  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday through Thursday: High pressure continues to build over \nthe Inland Northwest, bringing increasingly drying and warming \nconditions over the next couple days. Temperatures on Thursday will \nwarm into the mid to high 90s, with Moderate HeatRisk being observed \nacross much of the basin area, including the Spokane/CdA area. All \nthose sensitive to heat-related illnesses should make sure they have \nadequate access to cooling and hydration, and some systems and \nindustries will be impacted. On Thursday, unstable conditions \nbrought on by very warm temperatures and high lapse rates near 7 \ndegC/km will bring elevated fire weather concerns to the Cascades, \nespecially for already established wildfires. \n\nFriday through Sunday: While models are still working out details on \ntiming, there is increasing confidence that an incoming cold front \nwill bring chances for gusty winds and thunderstorms to the region. \nBefore the cold front moves through, temperatures rise into the high \n90s for most areas with isolated triple digits, leading to areas of \nMajor HeatRisk. With Major HeatRisk, anyone without adequate cooling \nand hydration as well as health systems and industries could see \nimpacts. \n\nFriday night into Saturday, moisture advection raising PWATs to 125 \nto 150 percent of normal ahead of the frontal passage on Saturday \ncombined with high temperatures and sufficient mixing will bring a \nchance for thunderstorms Friday night into Saturday. While these \nshowers will initially start out as isolated in nature, as higher \nPWATs increase in coverage, thunderstorms will become more scattered \nin nature, especially into later Saturday morning. Main impacts with \nthese thunderstorms will be lightning induced fire starts and \nisolated heavy downpours. Steep terrain and burn scars will need to \nbe monitored for any heavy rainfall rates. \n\nAdditionally, in the afternoon and evening, winds will increase with \nthe cold frontal passage, particularly across the Columbia Basin. \nThere is a 40 percent chance and higher for most locations in the \nregion to see wind gusts higher than 30 mph on Saturday night. Areas \nthat will see the highest gusts include the Lewiston, Moses Lake, \nWenatchee, and Waterville Plateau areas. These areas have a 40 \npercent chance of seeing wind gusts 35 mph or higher. This will \nbring elevated fire weather conditions to the area. Potential \nimpacts from these winds include rapid spreading of wildfires \nalongside blowing objects and difficulties driving, especially for \nhigh profile vehicles. \n\nBy Sunday, the low pressure will have weakened and moved out of the \narea, but breezy winds will continue to be seen across the basin, \nCascade, and Palouse areas through midday. A significant cooldown \ndue to the cold front will be observed Saturday into Sunday, with \nhigh temperatures dropping nearly ten degrees. This cooldown will \nresult in temperatures being a couple degrees below normal on \nSunday. Apart from the northern mountains and Cascades, \nprecipitation chances drop down to near zero.\n\nMonday through Wednesday: With high confidence (80 percent of \nclusters) indicating a strengthening ridge building over the area \nearly next week, the temperature drop will be short lived. By \nTuesday, temperatures will again warm into the high 80s to low 90s. \nChances for precipitation remain low, with PoPs 15 percent and lower \nexpected Monday through Wednesday. /AS",
